Sval: the domain policy decides an opaque origin by its maker, and matches a domain by scheme and host alone

SvalJavascript keeps per-site JavaScript rules in the script security
manager's domain policy (nsIDomainPolicy), the one mechanism Gecko reads when
it makes each global (xpc::Scriptability) and already broadcasts to content
processes; no chrome API decides scriptability per principal.

Once a policy was active, Gecko's DomainSet failed for three kinds of URI, and
Scriptability treats a failed check as "block script":
- a URI with no host (moz-nullprincipal:, so every sandboxed frame without
  allow-same-origin and every data: document): GetCanonicalClone's
  SetUserPass fails. Script died there with no rule at all.
- a host with a trailing dot: ContainsSuperDomain chopped it down to an empty
  host, whose SetHost fails.
- a page on a non-default port, or with a trailing dot, never matched its
  site's entry, so a rule did not apply to it.

A null principal's policy answer is its precursor's, as Sval's agent policy
treats such a frame as its maker's site (siteOfPrincipal), so a blocked page
cannot run script through a srcdoc or data: frame. A URI with no host that
has no precursor names no domain and belongs to no set. The canonical form
drops the port and a trailing dot, and the super-domain walk stops at the
last label. Every other failure still fails closed.

